+# Rules-Breaker Audit — Destructive Tool Guardrails (Schema-Level)
+
+**Cycle**: 2026-10-29 08:45+08, Job 477792, role: rules-breaker
+**Finding issue**: #2380
+**Method**: read-only schema audit — one `tools/list` read on the MCP Tower
+(`https://supremeai-mcp-tower.onrender.com/mcp`), inputSchemas of the 17-tool
+destructive/write family inspected verbatim. **Zero destructive tools were
+invoked**; no write, flush, wipe, purge, kill, suspend, deploy, or export call
+was executed at any point in this cycle.
+
+## Scope and why this lane is open
+
+The prior rules-breaker exposure audit (RULES_BREAKER_TOWER_EXPOSURE_2026-10-28,
+landed via #1845/#1850) explicitly recorded, at line 30, that the destructive
+tools "were never invoked — their gating is verified from the mirror source
+(secret/admin class) only". That classification answers *who may call* these
+tools; it does not answer *what the call contract itself demands once a caller
+is past the gate*. This report audits that remaining plane: the published
+inputSchema each caller sees — required fields, defaults, enums, and the
+presence or absence of in-call guards such as confirm tokens, dry-run flags,
+typed acknowledgements, or scoping requirements. The two planes are
+complementary, and both #2374 (memory write dedup) and the #1845 exposure lane
+are left untouched: zero overlap with either.
+
+## Findings
+
+### RB-1 — HIGH — `memory_wipe_memory` defaults to total erasure
+
+The schema declares **no required parameters**. Its only parameter is `scope`,
+an enum of `['all', 'graph']` described as "graph = knowledge graph only; all
+(default) = every memory lane". Two properties make this the highest-severity
+schema gap in the family. First, the maximally destructive value (`all`) is
+the **default**, so the least-typed call — an empty argument object — selects
+it. Second, there is no confirm token, no `dry_run`, and no typed
+acknowledgement anywhere in the schema; the erasure of every memory lane
+(facts, episodes, graph, tasks — the planes other agents demonstrably depend
+on, per #2374's memory probes and the task-recall tooling) is one bare call
+away from any caller who reaches the tool surface. The memory plane is
+load-bearing state, not cache: wipe is not a performance operation, it is
+amnesia.
+
+### RB-2 — HIGH — `action_redis_flush` makes "flush everything" the default path
+
+`required=[]`, with a single optional `prefix`: "Prefix to match keys. If
+empty, flushes all." The parameter that would narrow the blast radius is
+optional, and its **empty default is the widest blast radius available** — all
+keys under the configured Redis instance. Anything co-located in that Redis
+(rate/quota counters, rotation state as referenced by the redis_rotation tool
+family, session state) vanishes with the same bare call. The tool does carry
+`overrideRequestId` ("Approved Request ID to bypass policy engine"), which
+confirms a policy engine exists in the stack — yet the schema's base path
+still needs nothing at all.
+
+### RB-3 — MEDIUM — `action_cloudflare_purge` requires no arguments at all
+
+An empty argument object is a schema-valid call. Whatever the tool's server
+side defaults to (zone-wide cache purge is the plausible reading of a purge
+tool with no required zone or file list), the contract published to callers
+offers no scoping requirement and no confirmation step. Ranked below RB-1/RB-2
+only because cache purge is typically recoverable by re-warm, unlike memory
+wipe or Redis flush.
+
+### RB-4 — MEDIUM — `autonomy_kill_switch` and `autonomy_enable` are both zero-arg
+
+The emergency stop and its resume are symmetric, unparameterized calls: no
+reason field, no confirm, no actor identity beyond whatever transport-level
+auth carries. An emergency stop that is trivially invocable is also trivially
+*accidentally* invocable — by a confused agent loop, a malformed retry, or a
+prompt-injected call — and the zero-arg `autonomy_enable` on the other side
+means the "switch" holds no in-schema state gate either. The kill switch's
+value is precisely that it should cost something to pull.
+
+### RB-5 — MEDIUM — `memory_export_memory` completes the exfil-destroy chain
+
+`required=[]`, no redaction, no scoping, no target filter: one call returns
+the full graph. Standing alone this is a bulk-read convenience. Paired with
+RB-1 it becomes a **two-call chain — export then wipe — that both exfiltrates
+and destroys the same state**, leaving no local copy. Schema-level, nothing
+separates the two calls.
+
+## Positive controls observed (same audit, for balance)
+
+The family is not uniformly unguarded, and the contrast strengthens the
+finding: `redis_op` is enum-restricted to read-only operations
+(`ping`/`stats`/`read_key`) — it is *not* an arbitrary-exec surface and is
+excluded from the destructive class; `supabase_update` requires a `filter`
+string, foreclosing unbounded table updates; `slot_suspend`/`slot_revoke`
+accept `adminToken` and `reason` (audit context) parameters. Most tellingly,
+`action_render_deploy`, `action_redis_flush`, and `action_cloudflare_purge`
+all expose `overrideRequestId` — an explicit policy-engine bypass parameter.
+The bypass's existence proves the guardrail layer exists in the stack; what is
+missing is a **default-path guard** on the four tools where the default
+argument set is the destructive one.
+
+## Limitation (disclosed)
+
+This audit is schema-level and claims exactly that: the **published call
+contract** lacks default-path guards. Server-side policy gating may
+invisibly compensate (the `overrideRequestId` pattern suggests a policy
+engine mediates at least some tools), and verifying that live would require
+privileged calls outside this cycle's zero-credential, zero-destructive
+scope. Nothing here asserts the policy engine is absent — only that a caller
+reading the tool surface is not told to bring a confirmation, a scope, or a
+dry-run before destroying shared state.
+
+## Recommendations (spec-only; no code in this cycle)
+
+1. **Confirm token + `dry_run` default-true** for every `required=[]`
+   destructive tool (RB-1, RB-2, RB-3, RB-4): a call that would destroy
+   shared state should be schema-invalid until the caller passes an explicit
+   acknowledgement, and a default-dry-run gives every caller a safe first
+   invocation.
+2. **Invert `redis_flush`'s default**: reject empty `prefix` (or require
+   `prefix` as a required parameter); "all keys" should be an explicit,
+   spelled-out choice, never the silent default.
+3. **Scope `memory_wipe_memory`'s default to `graph`** and require an explicit
+   `scope=all` plus a confirm token for the full lane; ideally add a
+   session/lane filter so blast radius is choosable downward, never upward.
+4. **Parameterize the kill switch**: require `reason` (audit context) and a
+   distinct confirm token on `autonomy_kill_switch`; consider whether
+   `autonomy_enable` should require a stronger, separate authority than the
+   stop.
+5. **Apply the existing pattern**: the `overrideRequestId` bypass shows the
+   policy engine integrates per-tool; extend the same integration so the
+   default path of wipe/flush/purge/kill requires policy approval rather than
+   offering the bypass as the only schema-visible knob.
+
+## Audit disclosure
+
+Total Tower calls this cycle: one `tools/list` read (schema pull), plus
+queue/feedback reads and this submission — no destructive tool invoked, no
+live wipe/flush/purge/kill/export, no fuzzing or load, no credential use.
+Raw schema evidence archived at `scripts/rb_guardrail_evidence.json` in the
+agent workspace (17 tools, verbatim, incl. descriptions and enums).
